# The rock cube scene (T0157.3).
#
# **Written by hand from `10-scene-file-format.md`, never saved out of code.**
# That distinction is the whole point of this file: saving a scene and loading it
# back proves the format round-trips, which T0022 already tests. Typing one
# proves it is *authorable*, which nothing tested until this file existed — every
# caller of `loadSceneFromString` was in `tests/`.
#
# Three entities, one of each thing a scene needs: something to look through,
# something to look at, and something to light it.
#
# ## Coordinates, since every number below depends on them
#
# The camera looks along **-Z** with an identity rotation, +Y is up, and one unit
# is one metre — glTF's convention, and Blender's, since T0165 made the engine
# right-handed (`hp/HandednessConvention.hpp`, D33 as amended). The cube is 2 m
# on a side, centred on the origin in its own space
# (`samples/rockcube/content/models/cube.gltf`), so at `position: [0, 0, -5]` its
# near face is 4 m from the camera plane.
#
# **Every Z below changed sign with that decision, and the two quaternions were
# re-solved rather than sign-flipped by hand** — a quaternion is not a coordinate
# and negating a component of one is not the same as mirroring what it does. The
# derivations are below; they were checked by reproducing the *old* numbers from
# the *old* quaternions first, which is the only way to know the model of
# `resolvePlacement` used to solve them is the one the engine implements.
#
# ## What had to be computed rather than written
#
# Both rotations are **quaternions**, because that is what `Transform::rotation`
# is and the format has no Euler affordance. Neither was guessed:
#
#   * the sun's quaternion produces a light travelling
#     **`(0.15, -0.62, -0.77)`** — from above, from the camera's side, slightly
#     from the -X side, and **nearly frontal to the camera's view axis**. The
#     light convention is glTF's negative-Z (see `hp/Light.hpp`), which now
#     agrees with the camera's: an identity rotation points a lamp the same way
#     the camera looks, so a key light aimed roughly forward is the *default*
#     rather than something to compensate for. Only the Z component moved from
#     the pre-T0165 value; the key still arrives from above and from screen
#     left, so the look the reviewed frame had is the look this produces.
#
#     *(Corrected 2026-08-06 on T0159. This bullet used to claim the value was
#     `Rot(Y, pi - 0.7) * Rot(X, -0.7)`, travelling `(-0.49, -0.64, 0.59)` —
#     that composition produces a different quaternion than the one below, and
#     the discrepancy was found the hard way, by solving for the light a
#     shader was actually receiving. The N.L figures in the Sun's own comment
#     match the direction stated here, so the scene renders the look that was
#     reviewed and approved; only this derivation note was wrong. A frontal
#     key also matters to anyone adding surface shading: shadows cast away
#     from the viewer, so parallax self-shadowing reads small from this
#     camera except where a face turns until the key grazes it.)*;
#   * the camera's is `Rot(X, -atan(1.5 / 5))`, which pitches its view down by
#     16.7 degrees onto a cube 5 m away and 1.5 m below it. **The sign is
#     negative, and that is the derivation and not a typo:** `Rot(X, a)` maps
#     forward `(0, 0, -1)` to `(0, sin a, -cos a)`, so a *positive* angle now
#     pitches up. Before T0165 forward was `(0, 0, 1)`, `Rot(X, a)` mapped it to
#     `(0, -sin a, cos a)`, and the positive angle was the one that looked down.
#     The quaternion's x component is the only thing that moved.
#
# Writing those two lines is T0157's clearest finding about the format. See the
# ticket's awkwardness log.
version: 1
entities:
  - guid: 1570000000000101
    name: Camera
    components:
      Transform:
        position: [0, 1.5, 0]
        rotation: [-0.145213, 0, 0, 0.9894]
        scale: [1, 1, 1]
      Camera:
        verticalFov: 1.0472
        nearPlane: 0.1
        farPlane: 1000

  - guid: 1570000000000102
    name: Sun
    components:
      Transform:
        # A directional light takes only its *direction* from the transform, so
        # this position is documentation rather than data — it says where the
        # lamp would be if it were one.
        #
        # **Placed against the cube's resting yaw, not in the abstract.** The
        # cube rests at 30 degrees, so the large face the camera sees points
        # left of the view axis; a key from the right lights a *narrow* side
        # face brilliantly and leaves the big one dim, which reads as the light
        # coming from behind. Measured N.L with this placement: front 0.74, top
        # 0.62, right 0.26 — the face you are looking at is the lit one.
        position: [-2, 4, -1]
        rotation: [-0.329552, -0.07973, 0, 0.940765]
        scale: [1, 1, 1]
      Light:
        type: Directional
        colour: [1, 0.96, 0.9]
        intensity: 60
        enabled: true

  - guid: 1570000000000104
    name: Fill
    components:
      Transform:
        position: [4, 2, -2]
        # `Rot` mapping forward (0, 0, -1) onto (-0.85, -0.20, -0.48) — light
        # travelling left, slightly down and *forward*, so it *arrives* from the
        # **right**, which is the side the key leaves dark. Its job is the
        # narrow side face, and the two lamps divide the cube rather than
        # competing. Only the Z component moved with T0165.
        rotation: [-0.116595, 0.495529, 0, 0.860731]
        scale: [1, 1, 1]
      Light:
        type: Directional
        # Cool, against the key's warmth. A fill that matches the key's colour
        # reads as the key being weaker rather than as a second source.
        colour: [0.62, 0.72, 0.9]
        # A fifth of the key. **This is not decoration and it is not "more
        # light" for its own sake.** There is no ambient or environment lighting
        # in this engine yet (T0087), so a surface facing away from the only
        # lamp receives exactly zero — and a normal map, which perturbs normals
        # per texel, pushes a large share of an already-dim face past N·L = 0
        # and clamps it to solid black. Measured: with the normal map and one
        # light, one whole face of this cube rendered pure black; without the
        # map, zero fully-black pixels. A second lamp is the honest stand-in
        # until T0087 gives the engine an environment to fall back on.
        intensity: 10
        enabled: true

  - guid: 1570000000000105
    name: GlassPane
    components:
      Transform:
        # **On the camera's view axis, in front of the cube.** The camera sits
        # at (0, 1.5, 0) pitched 16.7 degrees down, so the axis passes through
        # y = 0.48 at z = -3.4 — which is how this y was arrived at rather than
        # nudged into place. The cube's near face is at z = -4, so the pane has
        # 0.6 m of clearance behind it, which is what `contactRange` in
        # `glass.hpmat` is measured against. (The pre-T0165 file said 0.481 for
        # the mirrored placement; the ray was re-solved rather than transcribed,
        # and 0.480 is what it gives.)
        position: [0, 0.48, -3.4]
        rotation: [0, 0, 0, 1]
        # A slab, not a plane: the cube mesh scaled thin in z. **No new mesh
        # asset**, which is the point — a pane of glass is a cube 4 cm thick,
        # and adding a `.gltf` for it would be adding a file to avoid a number.
        # Back-face culling leaves exactly the near face drawing.
        #
        # 1.1 m wide by 0.9 m tall at 3.4 m covers about a quarter of the
        # frame: enough that the rock behind it is visibly displaced, small
        # enough that the cube's own silhouette is still visible around it.
        scale: [0.55, 0.45, 0.02]
      MeshRenderer:
        # The cube's mesh, reused. See `scale` above.
        mesh: 15700000000000c0
        materials: [15700000000000a2]
        layers: 1

  - guid: 1570000000000103
    name: RockCube
    components:
      Transform:
        position: [0, 0, -5]
        # A 30-degree resting yaw, `Rot(Y, 30deg)`, so the very first frame
        # shows two faces at an angle rather than one square face flat on. It
        # matters more than decoration with parallax: the march's offset is
        # proportional to the view vector's tangent-plane component, so a face
        # seen head-on displaces almost nothing and reads as flat. The spin
        # composes onto this rather than replacing it.
        rotation: [0, 0.258819, 0, 0.965926]
        scale: [1, 1, 1]
      MeshRenderer:
        mesh: 15700000000000c0
        # One entry, because the mesh has one surface. A model with five
        # materials wants five, indexed to match its own material list.
        materials: [15700000000000a1]
        layers: 1
      # **A component this engine has never heard of.** It belongs to the
      # gameplay module beside this file, which registers it as `RockCubeSpin`
      # before loading the scene — there is no separate path for gameplay types
      # (D23). Load this scene *without* that module and the subtree is
      # preserved verbatim as an unknown component and written back by the next
      # save, rather than being dropped.
      RockCubeSpin:
        axis: [0, 1, 0]
        degreesPerSecond: 24
